Start by pinning down the requirement: what is the limit keyed on (API key, user, IP, or tenant), is it a protection limit or a billing quota, and is a small overshoot acceptable. That answer determines the whole design, because exactness is what costs money here.

The centralised design keeps counters in Redis, one key per subject and window, updated atomically so concurrent instances cannot interleave a read-modify-write:

```csharp
// Atomic in Redis: no interleaving between the increment and the expiry.
private const string Script = @"
  local n = redis.call('INCR', KEYS[1])
  if n == 1 then redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], ARGV[1]) end
  return n";

public async Task<bool> IsAllowedAsync(string subject, int limit, TimeSpan window)
{
    var key = $"rl:{subject}:{DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.Ticks / window.Ticks}";
    var count = (long)await _redis.ScriptEvaluateAsync(
        Script, new RedisKey[] { key }, new RedisValue[] { (long)window.TotalMilliseconds });
    return count <= limit;
}
```

The trade-off is that every request now depends on Redis. At high request rates the usual production shape is two tiers: a local token bucket per instance for the common case, with periodic reconciliation against the shared counter, so Redis sees a fraction of the traffic and a Redis outage degrades to local limiting rather than to an outage of your own.

Decide explicitly what happens when the store is unreachable. Fail-open keeps the service available but removes the protection exactly when load is likely abnormal; fail-closed protects the backend but converts a cache failure into a full outage. Protection limiters usually fail open, billing quotas usually fail closed.
